WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.500
This is a story about Ireland.

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:10.790
And it’s a story about an event that you’ve
probably heard of, but might not know much

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:11.790
about.

00:00:11.790 --> 00:00:15.970
It’s usually called, quote, “the Irish
Potato Famine.”

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:20.359
In most history books, the Irish Potato Famine
doesn’t get a lot of attention.

00:00:20.359 --> 00:00:26.970
At most, it appears as a pretty simple story
to explain Irish migration abroad.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:32.830
In the beginning, Irish people depended on
the potato for food; then, there was a potato

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:38.949
disease; many Irish died, and others migrated
abroad on what they called quote “coffin

00:00:38.949 --> 00:00:44.120
ships” – to England, Scotland, Australia
and North America.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:47.580
But the story of the famine is a lot more
complicated – and a lot more disturbing

00:00:47.580 --> 00:00:48.680
– than that.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.620
It was much less “natural” and much more
“artificial” than you might think.

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:57.070
And it all goes back to a man named Thomas
Malthus.

00:00:57.070 --> 00:00:59.280
Malthus was a British economist, a very influential
one.

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.132
And in 1798, Malthus published a book called,
quote, “An Essay on the Principle of Population.”

00:01:01.132 --> 00:01:06.710
And in this book Malthus put forward an influential
idea, and a rather counterintuitive one.

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:12.120
Malthus said that when food production increased,
people were better off for a little.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:17.530
But that improvement led to an increase in
population, which eventually caused societal

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:23.180
problems: there were too many people, and
not enough food to go around.

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.770
Poverty and famine were the natural results.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:33.540
Like animals, Malthus said, humans had a certain
“carrying capacity.”

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:40.110
When it exceeded that carrying capacity, enough
people would die to return the population

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:41.730
to a natural equilibrium.

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:48.790
So in the nineteenth century, that so-called
“Malthusian” model became the conventional

00:01:48.790 --> 00:01:52.600
wisdom in European intellectuals.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:58.350
It led them to focus on what they called “overpopulation,”
and made them basically think of famine as

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:02.000
nature reducing population to a sustainable
level.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.530
Not that the Malthusian idea was really correct.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:09.289
No serious economist today will tell you that
having “too many people” is the reason

00:02:09.289 --> 00:02:12.319
people are poor or hungry.

00:02:12.319 --> 00:02:20.510
In 1981, the economist Amartya Sen explained
the real cause of most historical famines.

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:26.080
If you look at the historical record, Sen
said, a collective lack of food is never the

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:27.610
real problem.

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:32.160
The cause of famines – while it often has
something to do with a decline in agricultural

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:37.760
production – is more related to the unequal
DISTRIBUTION of food: there’s enough food

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:43.380
for everyone, but some people can’t exchange
what they have for that food.

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:49.470
Sen found that famine is usually not the result
of “too little food,” but of exploitation

00:02:49.470 --> 00:02:51.040
and unequal economic relations.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:52.040
That brings us to Ireland in the 1840s.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.040
Here you have a perfect example of exploitation
and unequal economic relations.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:57.360
Irish people were the “other” against
whom the British could define themselves:

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:01.459
they were viewed as lazy, barbaric, barely
human.

00:03:01.459 --> 00:03:08.349
Ireland itself was run as a colony for the
emerging class of British capitalists.

00:03:08.349 --> 00:03:14.180
While England was rapidly becoming the wealthiest
country in the world, Ireland was kept incredibly

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.180
poor.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:19.250
The population was almost entirely composed
of peasants.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:24.870
The large majority of ALL HOUSES were huts
made of mud.

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:31.629
And the land those peasants lived on was owned
almost entirely by absentee English landlords

00:03:31.629 --> 00:03:37.580
who never visited Ireland, and extracted exorbitant
amounts in rent.

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:43.410
Even the British government regarded these
landlords with disdain, calling them, quote,

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:49.420
“the most oppressive species of tyrant that
ever lent assistance to the destruction of

00:03:49.420 --> 00:03:51.010
a country.”

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.540
So most Irish people were on a treadmill of
extreme poverty.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.360
They were the most destitute people in all
of Europe.

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:04.941
“In many districts,” one government report
found, “their only food is the potato, their

00:04:04.941 --> 00:04:06.830
only beverage water.

00:04:06.830 --> 00:04:11.080
A bed or a blanket is a rare luxury.”

00:04:11.080 --> 00:04:15.300
And that part about the potato is especially
important.

00:04:15.300 --> 00:04:20.430
Ireland has a lot of fertile soil, and a topography
that is ideal for grazing cattle.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.979
But when the British came in and colonized
Ireland, they systematically took that land

00:04:25.979 --> 00:04:29.210
and used it to produce beef for the domestic
English market.

00:04:29.210 --> 00:04:30.590
So the Irish were pushed off their land, and
had to make do with the worst territory on

00:04:30.590 --> 00:04:31.590
the island.

00:04:31.590 --> 00:04:36.819
And so the economically poor Irish turned
to the potato: a versatile crop that could

00:04:36.819 --> 00:04:39.979
be grown in large numbers on bad soil.

00:04:39.979 --> 00:04:45.240
But while the potato was ADAPTABLE, it was
also really UNRELIABLE.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:50.560
There had been widespread potato failures
pretty much every other year in the nineteenth

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:51.560
century.

00:04:51.560 --> 00:04:59.589
And in the 1840s, a new potato disease, originally
from North America, arrived in Europe.

00:04:59.589 --> 00:05:06.680
Huge potato crop failures sprung up in northern
France, in Belgium, and in the Netherlands.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:12.659
But none of those places were as dependent
on the potato as a source of food as was Ireland.

00:05:12.659 --> 00:05:20.430
So when the disease got to Ireland in the
1840s, the results were devastating.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:28.250
In 1845, about a third of the Irish crop was
destroyed; by 1846, seventy five percent of

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:30.039
it was gone.

00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:34.259
Hunger spread rapidly, of a severity that
had never before been known.

00:05:34.259 --> 00:05:39.759
This period became known in Ireland as the
Drochshaol: “the bad life.”

00:05:39.759 --> 00:05:45.110
But as Amartya Sen observed, this wasn’t
just about a pure lack of food.

00:05:45.110 --> 00:05:51.869
In fact, during the entire period of the famine,
Ireland was continuing to export food OUT

00:05:51.869 --> 00:05:53.159
of the country.

00:05:53.159 --> 00:05:59.849
Enough cows, sheep, pigs, and grain were being
shipped out of Ireland to feed the Irish population

00:05:59.849 --> 00:06:00.869
twice over.

00:06:00.869 --> 00:06:04.770
But the British government refused to do anything
about it.

00:06:04.770 --> 00:06:09.129
Soon, the Irish began begging their British
overlords for relief.

00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:15.020
And early on, the Conservative Party government—then
the representatives of the old British aristocracy—had

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:18.050
a fairly sympathetic response.

00:06:18.050 --> 00:06:23.279
They bought some food from America and brought
it to Ireland, distributing it to the poor.

00:06:23.279 --> 00:06:29.370
But in 1846, a different party, the Whigs,
came to power in Britain.

00:06:29.370 --> 00:06:35.379
And the Whigs did not have the sort of aristocratic
paternalism that the Conservatives had.

00:06:35.379 --> 00:06:40.759
They were advocates of a philosophy called
“LAISSEZ-FAIRE,” the idea that the free

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:44.990
market, left alone, would solve virtually
any problem.

00:06:44.990 --> 00:06:50.120
So the person they put in charge of famine
relief in Ireland was a man named Charles

00:06:50.120 --> 00:06:51.120
Trevelyan.

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:56.389
Trevelyan had been a colonial administrator
in India, and he was a big believer in those

00:06:56.389 --> 00:06:58.990
Malthusian ideas about population.

00:06:58.990 --> 00:07:02.309
He didn’t really want to stop the starvation.

00:07:02.309 --> 00:07:06.800
He thought it was, well, NECESSARY.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:14.330
The famine, Trevelyan said, was, quote, “an
effective mechanism for reducing surplus population.”

00:07:14.330 --> 00:07:19.779
He said it was “the judgment of God” that
sent the famine “to teach the Irish a lesson.”

00:07:19.779 --> 00:07:27.259
“The real evil,” he said, “is not the
physical evil of the Famine, but the moral

00:07:27.259 --> 00:07:34.400
evil of the selfish, perverse and turbulent
character of the [Irish] people.”

00:07:34.400 --> 00:07:37.969
So Trevelyan’s solution to the famine was
to let it ride.

00:07:37.969 --> 00:07:42.469
The laissez-faire Whigs decided to change
nothing: they refused to stop the movement

00:07:42.469 --> 00:07:48.499
of food to England, and limited the amount
of food they offered to starving Irish people.

00:07:48.499 --> 00:07:54.379
The food that WAS being imported into Ireland
was largely used not to feed the Irish, but

00:07:54.379 --> 00:07:58.999
to feed the cattle being slaughtered for British
consumption.

00:07:58.999 --> 00:08:05.819
Even when they did offer some relief to Irish
peasants, they instituted moralistic tests,

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:11.090
requiring that recipients live as indentured
servants in a workhouse to improve their work

00:08:11.090 --> 00:08:12.459
ethic.

00:08:12.459 --> 00:08:18.990
The British even required that food only go
to people who owned almost no land—forcing

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:24.409
impoverished farmers to sell all the remaining
land they had to their landlords, many of

00:08:24.409 --> 00:08:27.050
whom lived in Britain.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.870
And so the famine only got worse.

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:36.430
Diseases of hunger like typhus, dysentery,
and cholera spread wildly, killing just as

00:08:36.430 --> 00:08:39.410
many people as the lack of food.

00:08:39.410 --> 00:08:46.140
Social structures broke down: ordinary people
turned in mass to begging, crime, prostitution.

00:08:46.140 --> 00:08:49.720
In western Ireland, some resorted to cannibalism.

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:55.070
The British, fearing a rebellion, sent army
troops instead of food.

00:08:55.070 --> 00:09:02.660
As news of the famine spread all over the
world, shiploads of food finally began arriving.

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:07.840
Americans of all kinds generously sent money
and food supplies over the Atlantic, only

00:09:07.840 --> 00:09:11.950
to find they had to pay to have their cargo
transferred to British ships and then unloaded

00:09:11.950 --> 00:09:12.950
in Ireland.

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.590
Even several Native Americans, of the Choctaw
people, donated to famine relief funds, just

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:21.610
a few years after they had experienced starvation
themselves on the Trail of Tears.

00:09:21.610 --> 00:09:26.070
It was a beautiful example of international
solidarity.

00:09:26.070 --> 00:09:31.720
But these donations weren’t enough to stop
ruthless exploitation by British landlords.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:37.740
Even when enough food was being imported,
it wasn’t getting to Irish people.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:40.800
Starvation and death continued.

00:09:40.800 --> 00:09:46.570
Impoverished Irish, with barely enough to
eat, were evicted from their homes.

00:09:46.570 --> 00:09:50.420
One priest remembered the scene of a mass
eviction:

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:56.150
“Seven hundred human beings were driven
from their homes in one day and set adrift

00:09:56.150 --> 00:10:03.460
on the world, to gratify the caprice of one
who, before God and man, probably deserved

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:08.440
less consideration than the last and least
of them.

00:10:08.440 --> 00:10:14.430
The horrid scenes I then witnessed, I must
remember all my life long.

00:10:14.430 --> 00:10:21.020
The wailing of women—the screams, the terror,
the consternation of children—the speechless

00:10:21.020 --> 00:10:29.650
agony of honest industrious men—wrung tears
of grief from all who saw them.”

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:35.270
By the time the famine began to end, in the
early 1850s, about twenty percent of the Irish

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:42.250
population—two million human beings—had
died of disease or hunger.

00:10:42.250 --> 00:10:45.370
Another two million had left for brighter
shores.

00:10:45.370 --> 00:10:50.990
Today, Ireland’s population is still lower
than it was in the 1840s.

00:10:50.990 --> 00:10:57.710
But Ireland has never forgotten the famine,
and the generosity that some showed to them.

00:10:57.710 --> 00:11:04.000
During COVID, when the people of the Navajo
nation were hit hard, Ireland repaid the kindness

00:11:04.000 --> 00:11:09.000
shown to them during the famine by raising
two million dollars for them.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:14.340
And Ireland has never forgotten those who
did not help them – the British government,

00:11:14.340 --> 00:11:19.500
and all those who valued profits over humanity.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.320
As the British historian A.J.P.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:28.760
Taylor would later write: “The English governing
class had the blood of two million Irish people

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:30.930
on their hands.

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.780
All Ireland was a Belsen.”

